insert into public.community_responses (challenge_id, display_name, title, body, is_sample, selected_label, selected_position)
  select id, $seed$Sample$seed$, $seed$Build the aqueduct, but start with the wells$seed$, $seed$I recommend an aqueduct from the hill spring as the city's long-term supply, with immediate repairs to the existing wells while it is built.

The wells are failing for two reasons: drought is lowering the water table, and in the lower districts wells sit beside latrines and drains. Fixing the wells treats the second problem but not the first. A growing city needs a supply that does not depend on local rainfall, and a protected spring in the hills is cleaner than anything near the city. The river is no answer: it passes the tanneries before it reaches us.

Fifteen miles is a long route, and the valley in between is the hardest part. Roman engineers usually followed the contours to keep a gentle downhill slope and used arches only where a valley could not be avoided, so I would ask surveyors for a route that minimises bridges. It will take years and far more money than the treasury holds, so I would seek a patron's gift and a contribution from the province, and charge private houses for connections.

The trade-off is time and cost: people are ill now. That is why the wells come first: deepen the best ones, close those nearest latrines, and build public cisterns. But only the aqueduct lets the city keep growing.$seed$, true, $seed$The Infrastructure Plan$seed$, 1
  from public.challenges c where slug = 'the-water-decision'
  and not exists (select 1 from public.community_responses r where r.challenge_id = c.id and r.is_sample and r.title = $seed$Build the aqueduct, but start with the wells$seed$);
insert into public.community_responses (challenge_id, display_name, title, body, is_sample, selected_label, selected_position)
  select id, $seed$Sample$seed$, $seed$Clean, protect and share the water we have$seed$, $seed$My recommendation is to make the existing supply safe and to manage demand, rather than commit the city to an aqueduct it cannot yet afford.

The illness is worst where wells are close to latrines and drains. That points to contamination, not only shortage. So the first measures are cheap and fast: close or deepen the wells nearest to waste, move latrines downhill of wells, cover the remaining wells, and build a drain to carry waste away from the lower districts. Tanneries should be moved downstream so the river does not get worse.

For quantity, every large roof (temples, the basilica, the baths) can feed a cistern. Water should be shared by priority: drinking fountains first, then the baths, with private use limited during dry summers.

An aqueduct would bring more water, but it would take years, cost more than the treasury holds, and need constant maintenance. If the city borrows or taxes heavily for it, the poorest districts, where people are ill now, may wait longest for help.

The risk of my plan is that it may not keep up if the city keeps growing quickly. I would review it after a few years and, if demand outgrows the cisterns, begin surveying for an aqueduct then, with the health measures already in place.$seed$, true, $seed$The Conservation Plan$seed$, 2
  from public.challenges c where slug = 'the-water-decision'
  and not exists (select 1 from public.community_responses r where r.challenge_id = c.id and r.is_sample and r.title = $seed$Clean, protect and share the water we have$seed$);
insert into public.community_responses (challenge_id, display_name, title, body, is_sample, selected_label, selected_position)
  select id, $seed$Sample$seed$, $seed$Health now, supply in stages$seed$, $seed$I would do three things at once, in order of urgency.

Within weeks: protect public health. Close the wells nearest the latrines, build drains in the lower districts, and set up public fountains supplied from the cleanest wells. This targets the likely cause of the illness at little cost.

Within a year: increase storage. Cisterns under public buildings and a regulation keeping tanneries downstream will stretch the supply through dry summers.

Over the next decade: build an aqueduct from the hill spring in stages. A survey first, to find a route that follows the hills and avoids deep crossings; then funding from a mix of patrons (who gain honour from public works), the province, and fees for private connections. The first water should go to fountains in the poorer districts, then the baths, then private houses.

The main trade-off is that the aqueduct ties the city to maintenance for generations. I would set aside part of the connection fees for upkeep from the start, so the system does not decay after the patron's money is spent.

I am least certain about the terrain. If the survey shows the valley crossing would be enormously expensive, I would extend the cisterns and look again at drawing river water upstream of the town.$seed$, true, $seed$The Integrated Plan$seed$, 3
  from public.challenges c where slug = 'the-water-decision'
  and not exists (select 1 from public.community_responses r where r.challenge_id = c.id and r.is_sample and r.title = $seed$Health now, supply in stages$seed$);
-- Day 2: The Outbreak
select public._save_challenge($seed${"number":2,"slug":"the-outbreak","title":"The Outbreak","summary":"A deadly illness is spreading through a river town and nobody agrees on why. Decide what to do now, and how to find out what is causing it.","subjects":["Epidemiology","Statistics","Governance"],"challenge_type":"investigate","research_minutes":40,"scenario_kind":"fictionalized_historical","scenario_note":"A fictional English river town in the mid-19th century. The town, people and numbers are invented; the methods of investigation are real.","role":{"title":"The Medical Officer","body":"The town council has just appointed you its first Medical Officer of Health. You have a small budget, two clerks, the authority to advise the council, and very little time."},"situation":"In ten days, a severe illness with violent diarrhoea and vomiting has struck about 140 people, and more than 40 have died. Cases are concentrated in a few streets near the river. Some councillors blame foul air from the tannery and the open sewer; others suspect the water. Two companies supply water to different parts of town, one drawing from the river below the sewer outfall and one from springs above the town, and many poorer households still use a public pump. The workhouse, with its own well, has had almost no cases.","final_task":"Recommend what the council should do now to protect the town, and explain how you will find out what is causing the outbreak. Defend your recommendations with evidence and reasoning, and be honest about what you do not yet know.","expectations":{"easy":"Recommend sensible immediate actions and explain how you would start investigating the cause.","moderate":"Weigh the competing explanations, propose how to test them with the evidence available, and justify immediate actions given the uncertainty.","hard":"Design an investigation that could distinguish the explanations (including what would change your mind), address confounding and small numbers, consider the political and economic costs of acting before you are certain, and defend your plan against its strongest objection."},"cards":[{"kind":"role","image":{"key":"ledger","alt":"An open case register with a quill pen and an oil lamp on a desk."},"direction":"A clerk's desk by lamplight: an open case register with tallies, a quill and an inkpot."},{"kind":"situation","image":{"key":"pump","alt":"A cast-iron public water pump on a cobbled street corner."},"direction":"A cast-iron public water pump on a cobbled corner of a 19th-century English town, brick terraces behind."},{"kind":"constraints","image":{"key":"street-map","alt":"A hand-drawn street map of a river town with small marks clustered around one street."},"direction":"A hand-drawn town plan with small marks clustered around one street and a dashed outline around a workhouse."},{"kind":"investigation","image":null,"direction":"No picture: the questions are the content."},{"kind":"decision","image":{"key":"pump","alt":"A cast-iron public water pump on a cobbled street corner."},"direction":"The same street pump, seen closer."}],"constraints":[{"label":"Lives at stake every day","detail":"Waiting for certainty has a cost measured in deaths.","icon":"heart-pulse"},{"label":"Disagreement about the cause","detail":"Respected people hold opposite views, and the evidence is incomplete.","icon":"scale"},{"label":"Limited data and staff","detail":"Records are patchy; you have two clerks and no laboratory.","icon":"notebook-pen"},{"label":"Powerful interests","detail":"A water company and the tannery owner sit on the council.","icon":"landmark"}],"investigation":[{"title":"Epidemiology","questions":[{"text":"How does an illness like this spread, and what pattern would each explanation produce?","priority":"essential"},{"text":"What can you learn from where and when people fell ill, and who did not?","priority":"exploratory"}]},{"title":"Statistics","questions":[{"text":"How would you compare illness rates between groups fairly?","priority":"essential"},{"text":"What could make a pattern misleading: confounding, small numbers, missing records?","priority":"exploratory"}]},{"title":"Governance","questions":[{"text":"What can you ask the council to do before the cause is certain, and how will you persuade them?","priority":"essential"},{"text":"Who bears the cost of each action, and how would you handle their objections?","priority":"exploratory"}]}],"reference":{"background":"The scenario is modelled on 19th-century cholera outbreaks. Cholera is caused by the bacterium Vibrio cholerae and spreads mainly through water or food contaminated with faeces from infected people. Symptoms can begin within hours to about five days of exposure; severe watery diarrhoea can kill through dehydration within hours if fluids are not replaced. (Oral rehydration therapy was developed in the 20th century.)\nIn the mid-19th century the dominant \"miasma\" theory held that disease came from foul air. John Snow argued from evidence that cholera was waterborne. In the 1854 Broad Street outbreak in London he mapped deaths around a public pump and persuaded the local authorities to remove its handle; he also noted that a nearby workhouse and brewery with their own water had few deaths. In his study of 1853-54 he compared death rates among households supplied by two water companies, one drawing from the Thames below London's sewage outfalls and one from above, finding far higher mortality among customers of the downstream company (a \"natural experiment\", because the companies' customers were otherwise mixed in the same streets).\nUseful investigative ideas: map cases and the time of onset (an epidemic curve); count cases per household by water source and compute rates, not just counts; look for exceptions (people far away who fell ill, people nearby who did not) and find out what they drank; compare groups similar in other respects (to control confounding such as poverty, crowding and proximity to the sewer, which correlate with both bad air and bad water); be aware that small numbers produce noisy rates and that records may be incomplete.\nPrecaution under uncertainty: closing a suspected pump or advising boiling water is cheap relative to the potential lives saved, even before the cause is proven, and stopping exposure can itself be a test (cases should fall after a few days if water is the cause, though outbreaks also decline on their own as people flee or susceptible people are exhausted, which complicates the inference).","core_knowledge":"Waterborne (faecal-oral) transmission versus the then-dominant miasma theory; cholera's incubation (WHO: about 12 hours to 5 days); comparing rates rather than counts; confounding (poverty, crowding and nearness to the sewer go with both bad air and bad water); natural experiments; acting under uncertainty.","uncertainties":"Numbers are small and records incomplete; outbreaks decline on their own, so a fall after an intervention is weak evidence by itself; households may draw water from more than one source.","investigation_map":"Essential: how an illness like this spreads and what pattern each theory predicts; how to compare groups fairly. Exploratory: what the council can do before certainty; who bears the costs. Statistics meets governance because evidence must be strong enough, and presented well enough, to move a divided council with conflicts of interest.","approaches":[{"title":"Act on the water now","summary":"Disable the public pump, warn customers of the downstream company, advise boiling, investigate in parallel.","strengths":"Low cost relative to lives at stake; acts on the strongest clue (the workhouse's own well).","weaknesses":"Angers people who depend on the pump; a fall in cases afterwards is weak proof by itself.","conditions":"Works if clean water can be supplied meanwhile."},{"title":"Investigate to decide","summary":"Collect cases by household, water source and onset date for a few days, compare rates, then act decisively.","strengths":"Builds the evidence needed to persuade a divided council.","weaknesses":"People may die while data is gathered unless no-cost precautions are taken at once.","conditions":"Works if the investigation is fast and paired with immediate advice."},{"title":"Cover both explanations","summary":"Cheap measures against both bad air and bad water, staged so they also test which explanation is right.","strengths":"Politically easier; can teach something if measures are staggered.","weaknesses":"Spreads limited resources; simultaneous measures can confuse the evidence.","conditions":"Works if measures are sequenced or targeted so their effects can be told apart."}],"misconceptions":["Comparing raw numbers of cases between groups of different sizes instead of rates.","Assuming that because cases cluster near the sewer, the smell must be the cause (proximity to the sewer correlates with both air and water exposure).","That nothing should be done until the cause is certain.","That a fall in cases after closing a pump proves the pump was the cause (outbreaks may decline on their own)."],"critical_reasoning":"A weak answer picks a cause and asserts it. A strong answer separates protective action now from the investigation, explains which evidence would distinguish the explanations (rates by water source, the workhouse with its own well, timing), deals with confounding and small numbers, and has a plan for persuading the council.","evidence_expectations":"Use of the scenario's clues (clustering, the workhouse, two companies with different intakes); proposals to compare rates by water source; awareness of incubation and timing; reference to historical investigations such as John Snow's or to basic epidemiology.","difficulty_guide":{"easy":"Sensible immediate actions and a clear first step for investigating the cause.","moderate":"Weighs the competing explanations, proposes how to test them with the available evidence, and justifies acting before certainty.","hard":"Designs an investigation that could distinguish the explanations and says what would change the writer's mind; handles confounding and small numbers; weighs the political and economic costs of acting early; answers the strongest objection."},"fairness_notes":"Naming John Snow is not required. A cautious plan that investigates first can score well if it limits harm in the meantime. Modern knowledge of cholera is acceptable; credit responses that also consider what was possible in the period.","evaluator_notes":"Do not require the user to name John Snow or to reach a specific answer. Strong responses separate immediate protective action from the investigation, explain how evidence would distinguish the explanations (comparison of rates by water source, attention to exceptions such as the workhouse, timing), deal with uncertainty and confounding, and handle the governance problem of persuading a divided council with conflicts of interest. Modern knowledge of cholera is acceptable; credit responses that also consider what could be done with the tools of the period. Penalise confident claims unsupported by the scenario (for example asserting the tannery is definitely innocent without evidence).","sources":[{"title":"UCLA Department of Epidemiology, John Snow site: the Broad Street outbreak","note":"The nearby workhouse had its own well and few cases; brewery workers drank beer and had no deaths.","url":"https://epi-snow.ph.ucla.edu/Stream2_BSPoutbreak_c.html","verified":true,"critical":true},{"title":"UCLA Department of Epidemiology, John Snow site: the 'Grand Experiment'","note":"Southwark & Vauxhall (intake below London's sewage) versus Lambeth (intake moved upstream in 1852): far higher cholera mortality among Southwark & Vauxhall customers.","url":"https://epi-snow.ph.ucla.edu/Stream3_GrandExperiment_d.html","verified":true,"critical":true},{"title":"World Health Organization, cholera fact sheet No. 107","note":"Symptoms 12 hours to 5 days after infection (checked in the Chinese-language edition).","url":"https://www.who.int/mediacentre/factsheets/fs107/zh/","verified":true,"critical":false},{"title":"John Snow, On the Mode of Communication of Cholera (2nd ed., 1855)","note":"The original account of both studies. Not checked directly.","verified":false,"critical":false}]},"quality":{"scores":{"intrigue":5,"responsibility":5,"research_depth":5,"integration":4,"reasoning":5,"researchability":5,"clarity":5,"articulation":5},"weaknesses":"Closely echoes the famous Broad Street case, so well-read users may recognise it; strong answers must still reason from the scenario's own evidence.","recommendation":"approved","assessed_by":"ai"}}$seed$::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('id', (select id from public.challenges where slug = 'the-outbreak')), null);
