insert into public.community_responses (challenge_id, display_name, title, body, is_sample, selected_label, selected_position)
  select id, $seed$Sample$seed$, $seed$To the depot, with a line in the snow$seed$, $seed$We go for the depot, together, with a firm rule about when to turn back.

The numbers favour it. Seventy miles at about ten miles a day is seven days, within both our food (18 days) and our fuel (14 days). Returning to base is 180 miles: eighteen days at that pace, more with the injured man, and our fuel runs out first. Waiting burns fuel and the season gets colder.

The risk is that the depot is gone. So we cut fuel use now (one hot meal a day, melting snow only for drinking) and set a rule: if we have not found it by day nine, we turn for base on whatever we have left. Everyone hears the rule before we leave.

The injured man rides on a sledge, which slows us, but leaving him is not an option I will consider. The two with frostbite rotate to the lighter sledge. I will tell the team the plan, the numbers and the turnaround rule plainly. Fear grows in silence.$seed$, true, $seed$Press On, With a Limit$seed$, 1
  from public.challenges c where slug = 'the-failing-expedition'
  and not exists (select 1 from public.community_responses r where r.challenge_id = c.id and r.is_sample and r.title = $seed$To the depot, with a line in the snow$seed$);
insert into public.community_responses (challenge_id, display_name, title, body, is_sample, selected_label, selected_position)
  select id, $seed$Sample$seed$, $seed$Send the strongest ahead$seed$, $seed$I would split the party: three of the fittest go to the depot light and fast, and return with fuel; the other five follow at the injured man's pace.

Fuel is our real limit. A light party could cover fifteen miles a day and reach the depot in five days. If the depot is there, they bring fuel back to meet the main group, which by then has covered half the distance. If it is not, they return with the bad news quickly, and we turn for base together with more time than if we had all walked there.

Splitting is dangerous, and history is full of parties that separated and never met again. We would agree a route, marker cairns and a meeting rule before leaving, and the advance party would carry its own tent and full equipment.

I would lead the slower group myself. The person asking others to take a risk should not take the easier role.$seed$, true, $seed$Split the Party$seed$, 2
  from public.challenges c where slug = 'the-failing-expedition'
  and not exists (select 1 from public.community_responses r where r.challenge_id = c.id and r.is_sample and r.title = $seed$Send the strongest ahead$seed$);
insert into public.community_responses (challenge_id, display_name, title, body, is_sample, selected_label, selected_position)
  select id, $seed$Sample$seed$, $seed$Back to the hut we know$seed$, $seed$I would turn for base. It is far, but it is certain, and certainty matters when lives depend on it.

The depot was laid last season. Snow, wind and ice may have buried it, and if we march seventy miles to find nothing, we will be more than a hundred miles from the hut with half our fuel gone. That is the disaster I most want to avoid.

The fuel arithmetic for the return is tight, so we must change it. From today: one hot meal a day, melt snow only for drinking, and leave behind every non-essential item to lighten the sledges and keep our pace above ten miles a day.

I would explain my reasoning to the team, and ask for objections before we leave. If the weather stays clear and we make good progress, we keep going; if it turns, we reconsider the depot.$seed$, true, $seed$Return to Base$seed$, 3
  from public.challenges c where slug = 'the-failing-expedition'
  and not exists (select 1 from public.community_responses r where r.challenge_id = c.id and r.is_sample and r.title = $seed$Back to the hut we know$seed$);
-- Day 5: The Energy Dilemma
select public._save_challenge($seed${"number":5,"slug":"the-energy-dilemma","title":"The Energy Dilemma","summary":"An island's only large power station is closing in six years. Decide what should replace it, keeping the lights on, bills affordable and emissions falling.","subjects":["Physics","Economics","Environmental Science"],"challenge_type":"design","research_minutes":60,"scenario_kind":"contemporary","scenario_note":"A hypothetical island region in the present day. The island and its figures are invented; the technologies, costs and trade-offs are real.","role":{"title":"Head of the Energy Authority","body":"You run the island's energy authority. The island's government will follow your recommendation, and its 400,000 residents will live with the result: their bills, their air and their winter evenings."},"situation":"The island's coal-fired power station supplies about 70% of its electricity and must close in six years: it is old, expensive to repair, and the island has committed to cutting emissions. Demand peaks on cold, still winter evenings, and is rising as homes switch to electric heating and cars. The island is windy, has some sun, a long coastline, and sits 60 miles from a mainland grid. Residents already pay more for electricity than mainland households.","final_task":"Recommend what should replace the power station by the time it closes, and defend your plan. Explain how it keeps power reliable through winter peaks, what it costs and who pays, and how it affects emissions.","expectations":{"easy":"Recommend a replacement and explain clearly why it would work for the island.","moderate":"Compare at least two options on reliability, cost and emissions, justify your recommendation, and explain its main trade-offs.","hard":"Evaluate a mix of options, address uncertainty (prices, demand growth, construction delays), explain how the system copes with a still, cold week, consider second-order effects (on jobs, land, the grid and future demand), and defend your plan against its strongest objection."},"cards":[{"kind":"role","image":{"key":"pylons","alt":"Electricity pylons and cables crossing open country."},"direction":"Electricity pylons and cables crossing open country towards a small town at dusk."},{"kind":"situation","image":{"key":"power-station","alt":"An old power station with tall chimneys and cooling towers beside the sea."},"direction":"An old coal power station with tall chimneys and cooling towers beside the sea."},{"kind":"constraints","image":{"key":"wind-coast","alt":"Wind turbines along a rocky coastline under a wide sky."},"direction":"Wind turbines on a rocky headland and offshore under a wide sky."},{"kind":"investigation","image":null,"direction":"No picture: the questions are the content."},{"kind":"decision","image":{"key":"wind-coast","alt":"Wind turbines along a rocky coastline under a wide sky."},"direction":"The turbines again, from the water."}],"constraints":[{"label":"Six years until closure","detail":"Whatever you choose must be built and working in time.","icon":"hourglass"},{"label":"Winter evening peaks","detail":"The highest demand comes when it is cold, dark and often still.","icon":"zap"},{"label":"Bills already high","detail":"Many households struggle to pay; investment costs reach them eventually.","icon":"coins"},{"label":"Emissions commitments","detail":"The island has promised to cut its emissions steeply.","icon":"leaf"}],"investigation":[{"title":"Physics","questions":[{"text":"How much electricity do wind turbines and solar panels produce on average, and how does that vary by hour and season?","priority":"essential"},{"text":"What can batteries, other storage and grid connections do, and for how long?","priority":"exploratory"}]},{"title":"Economics","questions":[{"text":"How do the costs of building and running each option compare, and who pays for them?","priority":"essential"},{"text":"How should you compare an option that is cheap to run but expensive to build with one that is the reverse?","priority":"exploratory"}]},{"title":"Environmental Science","questions":[{"text":"What are the emissions and land or sea impacts of each option, over its whole life?","priority":"essential"},{"text":"What happens to emissions as heating and transport switch to electricity?","priority":"exploratory"}]}],"reference":{"background":"Variability: wind and solar output depends on weather. In the United States, utility-scale solar plants averaged about 25% of their capacity and wind roughly 32-35% over 2014-2017 (EIA); solar output is much lower at high latitudes and in winter, and offshore sites are generally windier than onshore ones. Solar output is lowest in winter evenings, exactly when demand peaks in a cold climate. Extended cold, still periods (sometimes called \"Dunkelflaute\") are a key reliability challenge.\nStorage: lithium-ion batteries are well suited to shifting energy by hours (US installations averaged about 3 hours of discharge, and 4-hour systems are common) and to fast grid services, but are not economic for covering multi-day or seasonal gaps at scale today. Pumped hydro offers longer storage where geography allows. Hydrogen and other long-duration storage exist but are less mature and less efficient.\nFirm capacity: something must cover a still, cold week: dispatchable generation (gas turbines, which emit CO2 but can run rarely as backup; biomass; nuclear), an interconnector to a larger grid, demand flexibility, or long-duration storage. A system with mostly wind and solar plus a backup that runs rarely can cut emissions steeply while keeping reliability.\nInterconnectors: a subsea cable to the mainland shares resources and backup; costs are substantial and depend on length and capacity; projects can take many years to plan and build, and the island depends on mainland supply and prices during shortages.\nNuclear: large reactors have very low operating emissions but long construction times (recent units averaged about 10 years, and most ran late) and high capital costs; small modular reactors are promoted as faster and cheaper, but first-of-a-kind projects have often overrun, so a six-year timeline is very uncertain.\nGas: in the US in 2019, gas-fired generation emitted less than half the CO2 per MWh of coal (EIA), and combined-cycle plants about 436 g/kWh against about 915 g/kWh for coal in one long-term study; open-cycle gas turbines are cheaper to build and suitable for occasional peaks; gas depends on fuel imports and prices.\nEconomics: levelised cost (cost per unit of energy over a lifetime) favours wind and solar in many places today, but whole-system cost also includes backup, storage, grid upgrades and balancing. Capital-intensive options shift costs to the present and depend on interest rates; fuel-based options expose consumers to price volatility. Who pays (taxpayers, bill-payers, investors) and how costs are spread matter for fairness.\nDemand side: efficiency, insulation, smart charging, time-of-use pricing and flexible heating can reduce peaks; electrification of heat and transport raises total demand and winter peaks. Closing a plant affects local jobs; transition planning is a real consideration.","core_knowledge":"Capacity versus average output (capacity factor); hourly, daily and seasonal variability; what batteries, other storage, interconnectors and dispatchable plants each provide; levelised cost versus whole-system cost; who pays and how; emissions over a plant's life; electrification raising winter peaks.","uncertainties":"Fuel and equipment prices, interest rates, demand growth from heat pumps and electric cars, construction delays, and how often still, cold weeks occur.","investigation_map":"Essential: average output of wind and solar and its variation; what storage and grid connections can do and for how long; costs and who pays. Exploratory: life-cycle emissions; land and sea use; jobs at the closing plant. Physics meets economics because reliability on a still winter evening is where most of the cost lies; environmental goals constrain which firm backup is acceptable.","approaches":[{"title":"Renewables with firm backup","summary":"Large wind build-out with some solar, batteries for hours, and a gas plant kept for rare still, cold spells.","strengths":"Steep emissions cuts using the island's wind; achievable in six years.","weaknesses":"Still burns some gas; needs grid upgrades; build costs reach bills.","conditions":"Works if backup runs rarely and funding spreads costs fairly."},{"title":"Interconnector-led","summary":"A cable to the mainland plus local wind.","strengths":"Shares backup and weather diversity across a larger grid.","weaknesses":"Long planning and build times; dependence on mainland supply and prices.","conditions":"Works if the cable can be delivered on time, with a bridge plan if not."},{"title":"Nuclear","summary":"Firm low-carbon power from a new reactor.","strengths":"Reliable output with very low operating emissions.","weaknesses":"Very unlikely within six years; high capital cost; overrun risk.","conditions":"Only with a bridge solution until it is running."},{"title":"Demand first","summary":"Shrink and shift the peak through efficiency, flexible heating and smart charging before building supply.","strengths":"Every megawatt of peak avoided is one never built.","weaknesses":"Depends on many household decisions; cannot replace 70% of supply alone.","conditions":"Works alongside new supply, with yearly tracking."}],"misconceptions":["Treating installed capacity as average output (ignoring capacity factors).","Assuming batteries can cover multi-day still periods economically.","Assuming a new nuclear plant could reliably be built within six years.","Ignoring that electrification raises winter peak demand.","Comparing only generation cost while ignoring backup, storage and grid costs."],"critical_reasoning":"A weak answer replaces the plant with an equal 'capacity' of wind or solar, or relies on batteries for a week-long lull. A strong answer distinguishes capacity from output, plans explicitly for still, cold periods, deals with the six-year deadline, and weighs cost and fairness alongside emissions.","evidence_expectations":"Realistic capacity factors and storage durations; examples of interconnectors, gas peakers or demand response; reasoning about costs and timelines; acknowledgement of uncertainty.","difficulty_guide":{"easy":"A replacement plan with a clear reason it would keep the island supplied.","moderate":"Compares at least two options on reliability, cost and emissions, recommends one, and explains its main trade-offs.","hard":"Designs a mix; explains how it copes with a still, cold week; handles uncertainty and second-order effects (jobs, grid, rising demand); answers the strongest objection."},"fairness_notes":"Several plans can be well defended, including ones with a role for gas or nuclear. Do not reward agreement with any policy position; reward reasoning about physics, cost and reliability.","evaluator_notes":"Several plans can be well defended. Credit responses that address the winter peak and still periods explicitly, distinguish capacity from output, deal with the six-year timeline, and consider cost and who pays as well as emissions. Hard-level responses should handle uncertainty and second-order effects (jobs, grid, rising demand). Do not require precise figures; correct clear physical or economic errors.","sources":[{"title":"EIA, Today in Energy: utility-scale solar capacity factors","note":"US utility-scale solar PV averaged about 25% over 2014-2017.","url":"https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=39832","verified":true,"critical":true},{"title":"EIA, Electric Power Annual, Table 4.8.B: capacity factors","note":"US wind roughly 32-35% in 2014-2017.","url":"https://www.eia.gov/electricity/annual/html/epa_04_08_b.html","verified":true,"critical":true},{"title":"EIA, Today in Energy: battery storage duration","note":"US battery installations averaged about 3 hours of discharge; 4-hour systems are common.","url":"https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=51798","verified":true,"critical":true},{"title":"EIA, Today in Energy: CO2 per MWh from coal and gas","note":"2019: coal about 2,257 lb CO2/MWh; natural gas about 976 lb.","url":"https://www.eia.gov/todayinenergy/detail.php?id=48296","verified":true,"critical":true},{"title":"How long does it take to build a nuclear reactor? (Sustainability by Numbers)","note":"Summarises construction times, including the World Nuclear Industry Status Report average of about 10 years for 2014-2023.","url":"https://www.sustainabilitybynumbers.com/p/nuclear-construction-time","verified":true,"critical":false}]},"quality":{"scores":{"intrigue":4,"responsibility":5,"research_depth":5,"integration":5,"reasoning":5,"researchability":5,"clarity":5,"articulation":5},"weaknesses":"A familiar policy topic; users with strong prior views may write opinion rather than analysis. The evaluation guide guards against rewarding either side.","recommendation":"approved","assessed_by":"ai"}}$seed$::jsonb || jsonb_build_object('id', (select id from public.challenges where slug = 'the-energy-dilemma')), null);
